    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class (the other two are a base class and a helper). The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented address normalization for owner creation. The change satisfies the spec and all tests pass (`Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n**New file** \u2014 `AddressNormalizer.java` (a plain shared helper, mirroring the existing `TelephoneE164` pattern): `normalize(String)` trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands abbreviations per token (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). It's idempotent and returns `\"\"` for null/blank input so the required-field check treats it as blank.\n\n**Modified functions** in the `POST /api/owners` pipeline (`officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`):\n\n- `ValidateRequiredOwnerFields` \u2014 normalizes the address *before* the required-field check, so an address that is blank after normalization is rejected (400), and writes the normalized value back onto the published body so every downstream step stores and returns the canonical form. This is the natural home for it: normalization can't raise its own error (blank is a required-field error), and it's the only step that binds `@RequestBody`, so it must run first.\n- `EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold` \u2014 household duplicate detection now compares addresses via `AddressNormalizer.normalize(...)` on both the request and each existing owner (last-name comparison unchanged).\n- `AssignOwnerHousehold` \u2014 the shared `householdId` derivation and member back-fill likewise use the normalized address on both sides.\n\nBecause the stored address is normalized and existing owners are re-normalized at comparison time, every address comparison uses the one canonical form regardless of how the stored data was originally cased.\n\nThe three acceptance tests confirm the behaviours: whitespace/case (`\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`), abbreviation expansion (`\"7 elm ave\"` \u2192 `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"`), and blank-after-normalization rejection (`\"   \"` \u2192 400).",
